 Solution $:$
Look at the first stages. When you have $1$ small equilateral triangle, you have $3$ toothpicks. When you have $2$ small equilateral triangles, you have $5$ toothpicks. When you have $3$ small equilateral triangles, you have $7$ toothpicks. From this, we can make an observation. If the number of small triangles is $n$, and we want to express this number using just $n$, then the formula will be: $$ 3\times n + (3-2)$$ The first part is because each triangle needs three toothpicks. And the second part is because there is a minus $1$ left at the heart of each triangle.
[asy]
size(150);
defaultpen(linewidth(0.7));
void drawSquare(pair A){

draw((A.x + 0.1,A.y)--(A.x + 0.9,A.y));

draw((A.x,A.y + 0.1)--(A.x,A.y + 0.9));

draw((A.x + 1,A.y + 0.1)--(A.x + 1,A.y + 0.9));

draw((A.x + 0.1,A.y + 1)--(A.x + 0.9,A.y + 1));
}

int k = 0;

for(int i = 1; i <= 250; ++i){

drawSquare((k,0));

++k;
}

draw((k+0.1,0.5)--(k+0.9,0.5),EndArrow);

[/asy]
<$answer$>
So, the number of toothpicks required to create the arrangement for the $250$th stage can be expressed as:
$$ 3 \times 250 + (3 –2) = 750 +1 = 751 $$
Therefore, the answer is: The number of toothpicks required is $751$.

